1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,740
363 miles of water highway, eight years of construction, thousands of man hours of labor.

2
00:00:10,741 --> 00:00:17,720
But by the time it was done, 3,000 canal boats traveled the new corridor from Albany to Buffalo.

3
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:21,740
Now, the Erie Canal on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
In the woods off the interstate in western New York,

5
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:40,120
a narrow ditch surrounded by a tangle of weeds

6
00:00:40,121 --> 00:00:44,220
is all that remains here of one of America's greatest engineering achievements.

7
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:57,200
Celebrated in story and song,

8
00:00:57,201 --> 00:01:00,440
the Erie Canal began construction in 1817.

9
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:04,800
It was the first commercial link between New York and the American West.

10
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:09,940
Thousands of canal boats made the journey from the Hudson River to Lake Erie

11
00:01:09,941 --> 00:01:12,460
during the canal's 150-year history,

12
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:16,400
making the Erie Canal America's first superhighway.

13
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,560
Though the Erie Canal went through three separate rebuildings and upgrades,

14
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:25,240
it was the original canal of the early 19th century.

15
00:01:25,241 --> 00:01:28,560
40 feet wide and 4 feet deep

16
00:01:28,561 --> 00:01:30,360
that changed our world

17
00:01:30,361 --> 00:01:33,860
and consolidated the strength of a young nation.

18
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:38,340
And in a very real sense, it united the country

19
00:01:38,341 --> 00:01:42,160
in ways that no other event of its time could do.

20
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:46,340
It was indeed a world-famous engineering marvel.

21
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:50,780
It truly is the backbone of the Empire State.

22
00:01:50,781 --> 00:01:59,320
It led to the creation of the Empire State, helped New York become the financial capital of the world.

23
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:02,680
One analogy I like to make is that it's very much like the Internet today,

24
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:08,680
that people really viewed it as something that was amazing, that was unprecedented, that was very much changing their lives.

25
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:14,600
Almost as soon as the ink was dry on the U.S. Constitution,

26
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:20,160
the new nation began looking west to the vast resources and abundant land of the frontier.

27
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:24,680
Yet America faced an obstacle to its rapid expansion.

28
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,360
It was called the Appalachians.

29
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:34,580
The backbone of mountain that stretches from the southern tip of the Smokies to as far north as Maine

30
00:02:34,581 --> 00:02:39,000
had always been a barrier preventing the simple exchange of goods and services

31
00:02:39,001 --> 00:02:42,680
between the abundant West and the civilized East.

32
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:47,840
The American West was simply cut off from the Atlantic Coast cities.

33
00:02:47,841 --> 00:02:51,260
except at one location.

34
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:54,100
The Appalachian Mountains do a very curious thing

35
00:02:54,101 --> 00:02:56,340
coming from Alabama going northward.

36
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:00,360
When they get to Pennsylvania, they veer nearly east

37
00:03:00,361 --> 00:03:03,960
and then turn north again through what is now New England.

38
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:08,020
This twist in the Appalachian chain

39
00:03:08,021 --> 00:03:10,960
provides the one place in America where a major river,

40
00:03:10,961 --> 00:03:14,400
the Hudson, flows from west of the mountains to the Atlantic.

41
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:17,780
But the Hudson River runs north,

42
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:26,640
What was needed was a connection directly west through the Mohawk River Valley and beyond.

43
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:30,440
The best way to exploit the opening at Albany was a canal.

44
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:37,100
In the late 1700s, canals were the obvious method of moving freight and goods.

45
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:42,700
Water was and still is the cheapest form of transportation, most economically.

46
00:03:42,701 --> 00:03:48,020
When you look at the ton mile, it takes a lot less energy and your costs are a lot less.

47
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:49,476
It's a lot smoother.

48
00:03:49,477 --> 00:03:55,600
There are no potholes in water like there are in even our today paved roads, much less the old dirt roads.

49
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:05,020
Canals have been a part of humanity's technological legacy since the Egyptian Pharaoh Menes built a canal in 4000 B.C.

50
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:11,120
Today in Egypt, canals still link the Nile to towns and oases.

51
00:04:11,121 --> 00:04:17,120
It may have been in canals very much like these that the ancient Egyptians floated blocks

52
00:04:17,121 --> 00:04:22,940
of granite to build the pyramids.

53
00:04:22,941 --> 00:04:29,120
While all canals are essentially the same, China's Grand Canal is 2,500 years old.

54
00:04:29,121 --> 00:04:35,280
A thousand miles long, the water highway linked the emperor's capital in Beijing with the

55
00:04:35,281 --> 00:04:40,640
ocean-going trade of the China coast.

56
00:04:40,641 --> 00:04:47,640
Over 25 centuries old, China's Grand Canal is still used today for the very same purposes.

57
00:04:47,641 --> 00:04:53,640
Until the invention of the steam engine, canals were the most effective means on the planet

58
00:04:53,641 --> 00:04:58,640
for transporting goods and services.

59
00:04:58,641 --> 00:05:05,640
In 1800, New York City saw a canal as the key to its future.

60
00:05:05,641 --> 00:05:09,400
Today, the Big Apple may be the financial capital of America,

61
00:05:10,020 --> 00:05:13,220
but then, New York was just another port city.

62
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:17,680
Well, New York, at the time that the canal opened, was doing pretty well.

63
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:20,020
It was growing, the population was growing,

64
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:24,000
the tonnage going in and out of the harbor was increasing,

65
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:26,220
the value of the goods were increasing.

66
00:05:26,221 --> 00:05:29,420
The only trouble is that Philadelphia was still larger,

67
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:32,140
and New York was in very big competition with Philadelphia.

68
00:05:32,141 --> 00:05:39,940
In 1808, the New York state government began to seriously study the possibility of a western canal

69
00:05:39,941 --> 00:05:43,900
that would link the Hudson River ports of Manhattan to the Great Lakes.

70
00:05:45,740 --> 00:05:50,125
In 1809, a delegation from New York traveled to Washington, D.C.

71
00:05:50,126 --> 00:05:53,620
to propose federal funding for a canal to the west.

72
00:05:54,600 --> 00:06:00,920
They projected a budget of $5 million, but many claimed a canal would cost several times that amount.

73
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:06,700
They went to Washington to meet with Thomas Jefferson.

74
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:12,860
And he said, well, this is a great idea for fellows, but maybe you ought to come back in 100 years,

75
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:18,600
because what you're contemplating would bankrupt the entire nation, let alone the state.

76
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:27,480
But a feasibility study for a western waterway began almost in defiance of Jefferson's flat refusal.

77
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:34,100
The New York state government sent a canal committee to tour western New York to examine the area.

78
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:38,840
At the committee's head was a savvy politician named DeWitt Clinton.

79
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:41,940
DeWitt Clinton was a renaissance man.

80
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:45,080
He was a naturalist.

81
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:46,760
He was an educator.

82
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:47,800
He was a politician.

83
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:50,940
He even published things on fossils.

84
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:53,200
He was a very intelligent man.

85
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:57,160
But above all, he was a politician.

86
00:06:57,480 --> 00:07:05,320
He probably realized more than any other person that the political fortunes of the person that was able to make the canal his baby,

87
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:07,460
it would either rise and fall with him.

88
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:13,900
By 1817, DeWitt Clinton was governor of New York.

89
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:22,880
He championed the proposed canal, which was projected to cost more than twice as much as it cost the state to fight the entire war of 1812.

90
00:07:22,881 --> 00:07:32,060
The state representatives from Manhattan were adamantly opposed to the project they called Clinton's Ditch

91
00:07:32,061 --> 00:07:38,500
In 1817, the Canal Bill went before the New York legislature

92
00:07:38,501 --> 00:07:41,940
By a very narrow margin, the bill passed

93
00:07:41,941 --> 00:07:49,000
Despite political opposition and budget concerns, the Erie Canal had been approved

94
00:07:49,001 --> 00:07:54,000
But the political obstacles would be nothing compared to the physical obstacles.

95
00:07:54,001 --> 00:08:01,000
The canal would have to traverse 363 miles between Lake Erie and the Hudson River.

96
00:08:01,001 --> 00:08:06,000
In between were mountains, valleys, rivers and swamps.

97
00:08:06,001 --> 00:08:09,000
The idea of the canal was beautiful in its simplicity.

98
00:08:09,001 --> 00:08:16,000
The reality of the canal would be far more complex.

99
00:08:16,001 --> 00:08:24,100
The total cost for the Erie Canal construction was $7.5 million, equivalent of $4 billion in today's dollars.

100
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:27,420
The Erie Canal will return on Modern Marvels.

101
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:33,660
The engineering of the Erie Canal presented several problems.

102
00:08:34,680 --> 00:08:41,320
There are 363 miles between Lake Erie and the Hudson River at Albany, which leads directly to New York City.

103
00:08:42,280 --> 00:08:46,860
To make matters worse, Lake Erie is 565 feet higher than the Hudson River.

104
00:08:48,380 --> 00:08:53,220
European canal builders considered the task impossible for a young and backward nation

105
00:08:53,221 --> 00:08:56,400
and derided the canal as Clinton's folly.

106
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:01,600
The planning of the canal had begun in 1810,

107
00:09:01,601 --> 00:09:05,780
with a first journey by the state's canal commissioners across western New York.

108
00:09:05,781 --> 00:09:11,380
Surveyor James Geddes laid out a route from Lake Erie to Albany

109
00:09:11,381 --> 00:09:16,060
that would take advantage of an ancient geological rift that split the state of New York.

110
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:27,480
In a very real way, the Erie Canal began in western New York 12,000 years ago, geologically speaking.

111
00:09:27,481 --> 00:09:33,280
At one point, the Great Lakes exited over this Niagara escarpment

112
00:09:33,281 --> 00:09:39,840
and flowed almost due east to the Hudson River, down what is now the Mohawk River.

113
00:09:39,841 --> 00:09:47,280
And it was those escaping waters from the Great Lakes that actually cut the Mohawk River gorge.

114
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,440
But the glaciers of the Ice Age had also created nearly insurmountable problems for a canal.

115
00:09:55,220 --> 00:09:59,060
Variations in altitude, crosswise streams, deep valleys,

116
00:09:59,420 --> 00:10:02,780
and the 70-foot-high solid limestone Niagara Escarpment

117
00:10:02,781 --> 00:10:07,360
which 25 miles to the northwest creates Niagara Falls.

118
00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:13,040
Following this path would be a challenge for the designers of the canal.

119
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:18,420
James Geddes oversaw much of the canal's early planning.

120
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:25,160
Amos Eaton was another designer, a geologist who later founded Rensselaer Polytechnical Institute,

121
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:28,340
now one of America's premier engineering schools.

122
00:10:28,341 --> 00:10:32,900
and heading the entire project was Benjamin Wright,

123
00:10:33,220 --> 00:10:36,120
who has been called the father of American civil engineering.

124
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:41,220
None of these men were professionally trained engineers

125
00:10:41,221 --> 00:10:45,060
and they came to that position either as land surveyors,

126
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:47,900
oftentimes they were trained as mathematicians,

127
00:10:48,320 --> 00:10:50,520
oftentimes they were trained as scientists,

128
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:52,560
but none of them were trained as engineers.

129
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:56,380
And in fact, Benjamin Wright had no college education whatsoever,

130
00:10:56,381 --> 00:10:58,440
But for that matter, neither did the other two men.

131
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:01,820
But despite their lack of formal education,

132
00:11:02,180 --> 00:11:05,640
these men were clearly up to the task of designing and building the canal.

133
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:12,840
They were very intelligent, and they were hands-on kinds of guys, and they took a chance.

134
00:11:13,060 --> 00:11:15,062
Granted, there was no school of engineering.

135
00:11:15,063 --> 00:11:17,200
You couldn't get a degree in engineering, even.

136
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:20,380
But these guys were engineers in every sense of the word.

137
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:25,280
These canal builders decided that construction of the Erie Canal

138
00:11:25,281 --> 00:11:27,300
would start at the center of the proposed route.

139
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,500
Rather than begin at the rocky and difficult terminals

140
00:11:30,501 --> 00:11:31,600
of Buffalo or Albany,

141
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:35,400
work started on a wide, flat plain centered at Rome, New York.

142
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:41,320
Construction began on the 4th of July, 1817.

143
00:11:44,220 --> 00:11:46,720
Today, the site of the first shovel full of earth

144
00:11:46,721 --> 00:11:48,640
dug for the Erie Canal is situated

145
00:11:48,641 --> 00:11:50,900
at Erie Canal Village in Rome, New York,

146
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:54,000
a reconstructed 19th-century township.

147
00:11:55,280 --> 00:12:07,160
Here, costumed reenactors bring the old Erie Canal to life.

148
00:12:07,161 --> 00:12:12,320
One of the few remaining sections of the original Clinton's Ditch lies here alongside the more

149
00:12:12,321 --> 00:12:17,200
familiar widened canal of the mid-19th century.

150
00:12:17,201 --> 00:12:20,760
The design of the Erie Canal was shockingly simple.

151
00:12:20,761 --> 00:12:22,100
than its European counterparts,

152
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:24,880
the Erie would be a basic dirt ditch

153
00:12:24,881 --> 00:12:27,020
40 feet wide and 4 feet deep.

154
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:30,520
It was determined that 4 feet of water

155
00:12:30,521 --> 00:12:32,040
supplied just enough buoyancy

156
00:12:32,041 --> 00:12:33,740
for a fully loaded canal boat,

157
00:12:34,280 --> 00:12:36,160
and the canal builders didn't want to pay

158
00:12:36,161 --> 00:12:37,020
for anything deeper.

159
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:40,260
The canal boats would be powered by mules,

160
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:43,020
who pulled the boats forward along a towpath.

161
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,060
The Canal Commission contracted out

162
00:12:46,061 --> 00:12:47,440
the actual work to farmers

163
00:12:47,441 --> 00:12:48,960
who owned land along the route

164
00:12:48,961 --> 00:12:50,520
proposed by the canal designers.

165
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:59,680
and then thousands of men just carefully dug a ditch four feet deep 40 feet wide on the top and

166
00:12:59,681 --> 00:13:05,040
28 feet wide on the bottom so the old canal of the 1800s was just a dirt ditch that was filled

167
00:13:05,041 --> 00:13:10,820
with water essentially it was from time to time two three thousand perhaps four thousand men

168
00:13:10,821 --> 00:13:20,460
working away at from 50 to 75 cents a day to get this ditch built 363 miles across the state of

169
00:13:20,461 --> 00:13:21,161
New York.

170
00:13:21,162 --> 00:13:26,500
But the construction of the Erie Canal took more than just shovels and strong backs.

171
00:13:27,760 --> 00:13:33,380
The local farmers hired by the canal engineers applied old-fashioned ingenuity to develop

172
00:13:33,381 --> 00:13:34,948
innovative new tools.

173
00:13:34,949 --> 00:13:40,100
In Rome, farmers found that plowing a section of earth before digging

174
00:13:40,101 --> 00:13:41,911
made the work easier.

175
00:13:41,912 --> 00:13:48,120
Perhaps the most impressive device was the stump puller, a 16-foot-high,

176
00:13:48,121 --> 00:13:53,520
30-foot long set of wheels and pulleys that would yank massive tree stumps out of the way of the

177
00:13:53,521 --> 00:13:54,780
canal diggers.

178
00:13:54,781 --> 00:14:01,980
Here was this gigantic device that had two wheels, oh maybe 14 feet in diameter,

179
00:14:02,840 --> 00:14:09,940
perhaps 12 feet in diameter, and a long axle, several feet long, a rope or a cable of some type

180
00:14:09,941 --> 00:14:13,071
tied around that middle wheel to a team of animals.

181
00:14:13,072 --> 00:14:15,220
Then a chain was wrapped around the

182
00:14:15,221 --> 00:14:21,100
axle to the stump. So when the middle wheel turned, the axle cranked pretty good.

183
00:14:24,040 --> 00:14:29,200
With this device, a crew of seven men and four horses could pull out 40 stumps a day.

184
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:36,860
The enormity of the early canal builder's task has been discovered by engineering students

185
00:14:36,861 --> 00:14:38,740
at Schenectady's Union College.

186
00:14:38,741 --> 00:14:49,360
Here at the historic Lock 23, students are restoring a vintage Erie Canal site using only the tools of the original canal builders.

187
00:14:52,040 --> 00:14:55,620
We could come in here with the backcoats and scramble things around fairly quickly,

188
00:14:56,080 --> 00:14:59,880
but we want to teach the students what it was like to build this before you had the major backcoats.

189
00:15:00,220 --> 00:15:05,440
We've got students in their mechanics classes that are working with pulleys and mechanical advantage and stuff like that,

190
00:15:05,620 --> 00:15:07,240
and engineering hasn't changed a whole lot.

191
00:15:07,241 --> 00:15:11,240
The laws of physics hasn't changed.

192
00:15:11,241 --> 00:15:17,240
These Union College students are following in the footsteps of the original canal builders in more ways than one.

193
00:15:17,241 --> 00:15:23,240
For the Erie Canal has been called America's first school of engineering.

194
00:15:23,241 --> 00:15:27,240
Up to that point, there really wasn't...

195
00:15:27,241 --> 00:15:29,240
In America, there were no schools of engineering.

196
00:15:29,241 --> 00:15:42,240
Most of the, what you would call engineering works, engineering feats, were educated men making educated guesses or educated decisions about how to build.

197
00:15:42,241 --> 00:15:43,853
A lot of trial and error.

198
00:15:43,854 --> 00:15:48,240
If you built it and it fell down, you built it bigger the next time.

199
00:15:48,241 --> 00:15:51,240
So you get a difference in quality.

200
00:15:51,241 --> 00:15:57,240
With the winter freeze in 1817, construction on the canal stopped for the season.

201
00:15:58,120 --> 00:16:00,620
Fifteen miles of Erie Canal were completed.

202
00:16:01,340 --> 00:16:07,180
The first section of the flat and seamless waterway that would eventually connect Manhattan with Lake Erie.

203
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:11,400
There were only 348 more miles to go.

204
00:16:14,460 --> 00:16:19,160
The Erie Canal paid for itself through a collection of tolls within ten years.

205
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:22,560
It would be another 50 years before the tolls were abolished.

206
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:25,940
The Erie Canal will continue on Modern Marvels.

207
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:34,920
The first season of canal construction ended on a triumphant note in 1817.

208
00:16:35,700 --> 00:16:41,880
All political opposition to the canal evaporated as the vision of a water highway to the west became clear.

209
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:49,080
In the second year of construction, the canal commissioners began to hire laborers from the cities

210
00:16:49,081 --> 00:16:53,520
rather than rely on local farmers whose crops took precedent over canal building.

211
00:16:53,521 --> 00:17:01,580
But in the first year of construction, the canal builders were digging on a flat plain.

212
00:17:01,581 --> 00:17:07,640
They had yet to tangle with the canal's greatest adversary, geography.

213
00:17:07,641 --> 00:17:12,120
The first skirmish with geography took place almost immediately after construction started

214
00:17:12,121 --> 00:17:16,240
again in the spring of 1818.

215
00:17:16,241 --> 00:17:22,340
The canal builders ran into New York's great Montezuma swamp.

216
00:17:22,341 --> 00:17:27,100
worked waist-deep in the swamp, flinging out shovelfuls of mud from holes which immediately

217
00:17:27,101 --> 00:17:33,260
filled with mud again. Malaria carried by the mosquitoes spread rapidly through the

218
00:17:33,261 --> 00:17:34,139
workforce.

219
00:17:34,140 --> 00:17:40,640
It has been estimated that in a single season, over a thousand men died of

220
00:17:40,641 --> 00:17:46,912
the malaria epidemic that swept through the ranks of the ditch diggers.

221
00:17:46,913 --> 00:17:48,060
The salvation

222
00:17:48,061 --> 00:17:53,335
of the workforce and of the canal was the early onset of winter in 1818.

223
00:17:53,336 --> 00:17:55,020
Cold weather killed the

224
00:17:55,021 --> 00:18:00,240
mosquitoes and allowed the remaining workers to dig through the now frozen swamp and shore up the

225
00:18:00,241 --> 00:18:06,816
banks with wood pilings. Another battle was against water itself.

226
00:18:06,817 --> 00:18:08,940
The Erie Canal's path

227
00:18:08,941 --> 00:18:14,340
crossed dozens of streams and rivers, which had to be either incorporated in the canal or crossed

228
00:18:14,341 --> 00:18:15,041
over.

229
00:18:15,042 --> 00:18:22,000
The concept of an aqueduct, a bridge for water, was old when it was used by the

230
00:18:22,001 --> 00:18:22,966
ancient Romans.

231
00:18:22,967 --> 00:18:27,600
But the builders of the Erie Canal made good use of that ancient design.

232
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:34,620
At Skohari Crossing, remnants of the canal aqueduct still stand, and the longest of the

233
00:18:34,621 --> 00:18:40,520
Erie aqueducts used 950-foot arches to take the canal across 800 feet of river.

234
00:18:40,521 --> 00:18:48,680
As the canal progressed during its first year of construction, the crews were learning from each foot of progress.

235
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:54,740
You start seeing you've got repetition in labor, so you're building a skilled labor force.

236
00:18:55,280 --> 00:19:00,400
By the time your laborers have built four or five locks, their master craftsmen are building locks.

237
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:10,120
The canal moved forward slowly, halting each year during the winter freeze, beginning again with a spring thaw.

238
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:14,020
making its way mile by mile from Albany to Buffalo.

239
00:19:15,340 --> 00:19:18,380
The most difficult obstacle was saved for last,

240
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:21,960
a ridge of solid limestone at the town of Lockport.

241
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:25,840
Its geological name was the Niagara Escarpment.

242
00:19:28,740 --> 00:19:32,940
One of the challenges that the engineers had

243
00:19:32,941 --> 00:19:35,460
was getting over the Niagara Escarpment.

244
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:39,520
It held Lake Erie from entering New York State

245
00:19:39,521 --> 00:19:43,200
and to the north you'll find Niagara Falls, which is similar.

246
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,680
There's a 30-foot thick layer of limestone and flint.

247
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:50,480
The escarpment itself is 60 feet high.

248
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:54,100
The first problem facing the canal builders

249
00:19:54,101 --> 00:19:58,000
was the challenge of getting water to flow uphill for 60 feet.

250
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:03,000
The answer to this problem first appeared in the 13th century in Holland.

251
00:20:03,620 --> 00:20:05,380
It was called a lock.

252
00:20:05,381 --> 00:20:09,980
A lock is a stretch of canal enclosed by gates.

253
00:20:10,660 --> 00:20:15,140
When a boat enters, the gates are closed, and water is let into or drained out.

254
00:20:16,180 --> 00:20:21,680
When the water level reaches that of the new stretch of canal, the gates are opened and the boat moves out.

255
00:20:23,560 --> 00:20:27,780
But in 1823, when work began on the locks of the Niagara Escarpment,

256
00:20:28,340 --> 00:20:30,960
locks had been used only to raise a boat a few feet.

257
00:20:31,440 --> 00:20:34,840
No one had ever attempted to raise boats 60 feet.

258
00:20:35,380 --> 00:20:42,320
The canal engineers devised an ingenious flight of five locks in a series.

259
00:20:42,321 --> 00:20:47,420
Each lock would raise a boat 12 feet to the next lock, and finally to the top of the ridge

260
00:20:47,421 --> 00:20:49,420
and onto Lake Erie.

261
00:20:49,421 --> 00:20:57,060
The Lockport Locks still exist and work today, though they've been upgraded and modernized.

262
00:20:57,061 --> 00:21:03,840
Mike Murphy is a boat captain with a hands-on understanding of the canal and its locks.

263
00:21:03,841 --> 00:21:07,340
came down the Erie Canal. And by building the Erie Canal, it would open up the whole

264
00:21:07,341 --> 00:21:10,600
New York State and open up the whole Midwest.

265
00:21:11,860 --> 00:21:16,500
The famous locks going up the Niagara Escarpment were modernized in the 20th century

266
00:21:16,501 --> 00:21:20,680
and reduced from 5 to 2. Today's locks, we're going to go through

267
00:21:20,681 --> 00:21:21,767
our 5 stories high.

268
00:21:21,768 --> 00:21:26,000
They'll weigh 50 tons apiece and they're operated by a 7 horsepower motor.

269
00:21:26,760 --> 00:21:28,189
We're going to go up 25 feet.

270
00:21:28,190 --> 00:21:31,540
They'll be 3.5 million gallons of water flowing here all by gravity.

271
00:21:59,680 --> 00:22:02,260
This was an engineering marvel beyond compare.

272
00:22:02,261 --> 00:22:10,400
No less a person than the Marquis de Lafayette, when he returned to America in 1825 in triumph,

273
00:22:10,940 --> 00:22:14,820
stood at the locks and offered a toast to the locks at Lockport,

274
00:22:15,320 --> 00:22:21,340
said equal only to the wonder of equality and liberty in this country.

275
00:22:22,860 --> 00:22:27,540
Yet designing a set of locks and actually building them were quite different things.

276
00:22:28,700 --> 00:22:32,100
The canal builders still had to dig through the solid rock,

277
00:22:32,260 --> 00:22:37,800
And when the locks themselves were completed, the men had to dig and blast a three-mile

278
00:22:37,801 --> 00:22:40,380
canal corridor through the limestone.

279
00:22:40,381 --> 00:22:46,840
Well, this is a miniature version of what would have been used by the workmen to drill

280
00:22:46,841 --> 00:22:49,860
the blast holes for the lockport cut.

281
00:22:49,861 --> 00:22:57,900
They would have used a specially tempered steel rod held by one man, while the other

282
00:22:57,901 --> 00:23:01,300
man would hit the top of the rod with a sledgehammer.

283
00:23:01,301 --> 00:23:07,180
essentially strike, turn, strike, turn.

284
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:18,440
These holes would be filled with gunpowder and the rock would be blasted out.

285
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:25,540
Once the explosion had broken the rock, the men would smash it with sledgehammers and shovel it out.

286
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:35,320
But as the trench got deeper, wheelbarrows and brute strength were eventually replaced by the ingenious, homegrown creation of an inventor named Orange Dibble.

287
00:23:36,780 --> 00:23:43,700
He invented a derrick that could be operated by a single horse, but which could lift one ton of stone at a time.

288
00:23:44,460 --> 00:23:49,380
These derricks were placed every 70 feet along the west bank of the Lockport Cut.

289
00:23:49,381 --> 00:23:55,260
And so at the bottom of the cut, the men would blast and put debris in these buckets,

290
00:23:55,500 --> 00:23:59,080
and then the buckets would be lifted and dropped on the side of the canal.

291
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:06,140
The struggle to cut a trench through the Niagara escarpment took longer than anyone had anticipated.

292
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:10,660
Construction had begun on the Lockport Locks in 1823.

293
00:24:11,460 --> 00:24:14,580
It wasn't completed until autumn of 1825.

294
00:24:14,581 --> 00:24:21,880
And it was in fact that last bit of work in the Lockport cut that delayed the opening of the canal,

295
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:30,260
and it was that last piece of work when finished that signaled the opening of the entire Erie Canal from Buffalo to New York City.

296
00:24:32,600 --> 00:24:37,460
After eight years and seven million dollars, the Erie Canal was completed.

297
00:24:37,461 --> 00:24:47,480
On October 26, 1825, Governor DeWitt Clinton ceremoniously boarded a canal boat named the Seneca Chief at Buffalo

298
00:24:47,481 --> 00:24:51,880
and began the triumphant journey down the entire length of the Erie Canal.

299
00:24:53,540 --> 00:24:55,491
Clinton's passage took nine days.

300
00:24:55,492 --> 00:24:58,920
Parades and festivals accompanied him all along the route.

301
00:24:59,720 --> 00:25:06,480
Clinton's ditch was toasted in almost every village and town along the 363 miles of the canal.

302
00:25:07,460 --> 00:25:09,760
Not everyone was happy with this.

303
00:25:10,460 --> 00:25:13,080
At Rome, for example, when they built Clinton's Ditch,

304
00:25:13,180 --> 00:25:14,820
they diverted it south of the city.

305
00:25:15,020 --> 00:25:16,060
People didn't like that, you know?

306
00:25:16,700 --> 00:25:19,600
So when the governor came through on the boat,

307
00:25:19,840 --> 00:25:23,160
they met him there with a keg of water from the old Rome Canal.

308
00:25:23,940 --> 00:25:27,340
The keg of water, the barrel, was draped in black.

309
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:31,320
And as he went by, they dumped the thing in front of his boat,

310
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:33,020
you know, as a sign of protest.

311
00:25:33,021 --> 00:25:41,440
Clinton's ceremonial regatta entered the Hudson River and the canal's terminus on November 4, 1825.

312
00:25:42,780 --> 00:25:46,640
From there, the flotilla continued directly to the harbor of New York.

313
00:25:47,580 --> 00:25:51,220
Governor Clinton poured a barrel of water from Lake Erie into the Atlantic Ocean

314
00:25:51,221 --> 00:25:54,260
in a ceremony called the Wedding of the Waters.

315
00:25:56,060 --> 00:25:58,660
It symbolized the grand hopes for the canal

316
00:25:58,661 --> 00:26:02,360
that finally linked the Atlantic coast to the riches of the West.

317
00:26:03,020 --> 00:26:07,720
The Erie Canal was open. Now the real work could begin.

318
00:26:10,600 --> 00:26:15,580
Ministers across New York lobbied to have the Erie Canal legally closed on the Sabbath.

319
00:26:16,280 --> 00:26:20,480
They failed. The Erie Canal will continue on Modern Marvels.

320
00:26:22,660 --> 00:26:26,960
By 1826, the first full year of Erie Canal operation,

321
00:26:27,720 --> 00:26:33,360
160 canal boats were making their way on the six-day journey from Albany to Buffalo and back again.

322
00:26:34,220 --> 00:26:36,300
It was a triumph for a young nation.

323
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:40,080
With the opening of the Erie Canal,

324
00:26:40,700 --> 00:26:45,560
here was something that not only New Yorkers, who had paid for the canal, could be proud of,

325
00:26:45,860 --> 00:26:48,100
but the whole nation was proud of this canal.

326
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,720
When the canal first opened, people from all over the world,

327
00:26:52,820 --> 00:26:56,120
but particularly from the eastern United States, came up to see it.

328
00:26:56,121 --> 00:26:59,240
It was touted as being this amazing achievement.

329
00:26:59,720 --> 00:27:04,080
There were technological feats that people thought would be impossible to accomplish,

330
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:05,800
and so people went out to see it.

331
00:27:07,820 --> 00:27:13,140
The Erie Canal was not only an engineering success, it was an economic success as well.

332
00:27:14,080 --> 00:27:20,860
The economic prosperity of New York was extremely retarded by the lack of markets,

333
00:27:21,480 --> 00:27:24,800
by the lack of settlers coming in and settling the land.

334
00:27:24,801 --> 00:27:29,540
That literally changed almost overnight with the opening of the Erie Canal.

335
00:27:30,180 --> 00:27:35,940
What had cost $100 a ton to transport to eastern markets prior to the canal

336
00:27:35,941 --> 00:27:39,580
suddenly cost only $8 to $10 per ton.

337
00:27:40,480 --> 00:27:46,980
It was the most successful, the largest public works project this nation had ever seen.

338
00:27:47,940 --> 00:27:52,840
And it showed a profit so large that it offset the state budget by two-thirds.

339
00:27:54,800 --> 00:27:58,000
The basic unit of canal commerce was the canal boat.

340
00:27:59,040 --> 00:28:04,020
Based upon European models, they were usually 14 feet wide by 78 feet long,

341
00:28:04,580 --> 00:28:06,560
essentially a box that floated.

342
00:28:07,900 --> 00:28:12,560
Each of the flat-bottomed canal craft could carry 100,000 pounds of freight,

343
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:15,720
wheat, lumber and raw materials from the west,

344
00:28:16,340 --> 00:28:19,600
furniture, tools, clothes and manufactured goods from the east.

345
00:28:19,601 --> 00:28:30,220
At nearly a half cent per ton per mile, a boatload of flour would pay a toll of $165 for the entire trip.

346
00:28:30,920 --> 00:28:34,220
A good sum in those days for the coffers of New York State.

347
00:28:36,160 --> 00:28:41,620
Even with the closure of the canal every winter, revenues from the Erie made New York State wealthy.

348
00:28:43,960 --> 00:28:49,340
Canal boats carried passengers too, and some, called packet boats, carried only passengers.

349
00:28:49,600 --> 00:28:56,600
Some of them were tourists, some were traveling for business, some were pioneers moving west.

350
00:28:56,601 --> 00:29:01,600
The six day journey across the length of the canal was slow.

351
00:29:01,601 --> 00:29:05,600
The legal speed limit on the canal was four miles an hour.

352
00:29:05,601 --> 00:29:10,600
During the day, the packet boat's central cabin served as a sitting room,

353
00:29:10,601 --> 00:29:14,600
and some pilots hired musicians to serenade the passengers.

354
00:29:14,601 --> 00:29:19,400
Packet boats traveled all day and all night without stopping.

355
00:29:20,360 --> 00:29:25,020
The boats had bathrooms at each end, many with an ornately designed chamber pot.

356
00:29:26,900 --> 00:29:31,480
A crew member would later take the pot and empty it directly into the canal.

357
00:29:36,720 --> 00:29:40,260
At mealtimes, the packet boat cabins became dining rooms,

358
00:29:40,261 --> 00:29:44,880
and the most expensive boats served elegant meals on linen tablecloths.

359
00:29:45,580 --> 00:29:50,360
At night, the cabins transformed to sleeping rooms with pull-down berths.

360
00:29:51,180 --> 00:29:54,620
The sexes were segregated when it came time to sleep.

361
00:29:55,140 --> 00:29:59,900
Men and boys would be on one side of the curtain and women would be on the other side.

362
00:29:59,901 --> 00:30:03,020
So almost every canal boat would have a curtain like this,

363
00:30:03,560 --> 00:30:06,400
which would keep things decent during the nighttime.

364
00:30:06,401 --> 00:30:15,160
A packet boat could sleep 40 people as it made its way 4 miles an hour, 24 hours a day across the state.

365
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:23,540
Often passengers would sit on the wide, comfortable roof of the canal boat and watch the city and countryside roll by.

366
00:30:25,280 --> 00:30:27,140
There was a danger to this, though.

367
00:30:27,960 --> 00:30:34,900
Even at 4 miles per hour, the bridges over the Erie Canal were built only 4 feet high in order to save money.

368
00:30:34,901 --> 00:30:44,560
Hence the famous warning cry, Low Bridge, Everybody Down, to remind passengers to literally duck as they passed under the bridge.

369
00:30:53,560 --> 00:30:58,760
Another common element of the canal celebrated in story and song was the canal mule.

370
00:31:04,900 --> 00:31:13,360
The thing that made the canal so successful was the fact that two animals this size could carry loads as great as 75 ton on a canal boat,

371
00:31:13,960 --> 00:31:16,180
as opposed to just a few ton on a wagon.

372
00:31:17,080 --> 00:31:20,200
The canal proved to be so successful, the state made it wider and deeper,

373
00:31:20,201 --> 00:31:26,460
and boats got upwards of 250 ton loads, and they'd use four and sometimes five mules to pull those boats.

374
00:31:26,461 --> 00:31:29,680
From over need to above.

375
00:31:32,300 --> 00:31:39,940
Mules, despite their usefulness, had a reputation for stubbornness, unpredictability, danger, and stench.

376
00:31:40,440 --> 00:31:43,180
Not unlike the reputation of the canal men themselves.

377
00:31:44,600 --> 00:31:46,740
The canalers were a rough bunch.

378
00:31:46,741 --> 00:31:52,740
The freight boat captains were at constant war with the captains of the lumber rafts

379
00:31:52,741 --> 00:31:59,100
because the lumber rafts took so much longer to lock through the locks that it held up the freight boats.

380
00:31:59,720 --> 00:32:05,640
And so fistfights were very common, and not a few killings took place between the canalers.

381
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:12,840
The Erie Canal became an entire industry and a way of life.

382
00:32:13,360 --> 00:32:18,600
One estimate from 1840 said 30,000 men, women, and children worked on the canal.

383
00:32:18,601 --> 00:32:25,080
There were not only boat crews, but lock tenders, canal repairmen, boat builders, toll takers,

384
00:32:25,240 --> 00:32:30,300
as well as the auxiliary vocations of bartender, innkeeper, and prostitute.

385
00:32:30,980 --> 00:32:33,680
New jobs and new terms entered the vocabulary.

386
00:32:34,440 --> 00:32:39,940
The crews who unloaded the canal boats were called a longshoreman, the root of the word longshoreman.

387
00:32:41,440 --> 00:32:47,300
A hoagie was an old Scottish word, used for the boys who were hired to lead the canal mules along the towpath.

388
00:32:48,600 --> 00:32:52,000
It was a difficult and often brutal job for a child.

389
00:32:53,420 --> 00:32:55,400
They were not treated with very much respect.

390
00:32:55,780 --> 00:33:00,320
The boys who worked along the canal were often sheltered in barns alongside their horses,

391
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:02,860
often with just four or five hours of sleep a night.

392
00:33:03,220 --> 00:33:06,740
And then they would pull a boat and then they would stop and maybe have a few more hours of sleep.

393
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:11,860
With the canal and the canal workers came new towns and new settlements.

394
00:33:11,861 --> 00:33:21,160
Rochester exploded in 1810 when DeWitt Clinton came here on the exploratory trip with the canal commissioners and James Geddes.

395
00:33:21,540 --> 00:33:24,520
There wasn't one person living in what is now downtown Rochester.

396
00:33:25,320 --> 00:33:26,160
It was 1810.

397
00:33:27,200 --> 00:33:30,300
By 1825, there were thousands of people.

398
00:33:32,040 --> 00:33:37,980
The Erie Canal not only affected western New York, but it had a crucial effect on New York City as well.

399
00:33:38,840 --> 00:33:43,400
It was the steady shipments of flour and grain on the Erie Canal that made New York

400
00:33:43,401 --> 00:33:48,600
the prime port of the United States and the capital of commerce for the entire nation.

401
00:33:50,460 --> 00:33:55,760
Another small fact that I loved was in the first six months of the opening of the canal,

402
00:33:55,761 --> 00:34:02,000
there were over 1,500 advertisements, new advertisements in the local papers

403
00:34:02,001 --> 00:34:05,840
that were related to the goods that were shipping through the canal.

404
00:34:05,841 --> 00:34:10,180
Despite the canal's success, there were those who were against it.

405
00:34:11,380 --> 00:34:14,160
Nathaniel Hawthorne, author of The Scarlet Letter,

406
00:34:14,760 --> 00:34:17,860
said the Erie Canal was an environmental disaster

407
00:34:17,861 --> 00:34:20,620
that ruined the pristine wilderness of New York.

408
00:34:21,900 --> 00:34:24,460
Farmers had land expropriated to build locks

409
00:34:24,461 --> 00:34:26,920
or had their farms cut in two by the canal.

410
00:34:28,560 --> 00:34:31,720
Others felt that the transformations wrought by the Erie Canal

411
00:34:31,721 --> 00:34:35,940
made life in society too speedy, stressful, and complicated.

412
00:34:37,560 --> 00:34:42,260
Some people just didn't like the whole market revolution that was happening at the time.

413
00:34:42,420 --> 00:34:43,660
They were suspicious of it.

414
00:34:43,720 --> 00:34:46,520
They thought that it was leading to class divisions in society.

415
00:34:46,940 --> 00:34:49,040
They thought that it was making people too materialistic.

416
00:34:50,520 --> 00:34:53,600
Whether an individual approved or disapproved of it,

417
00:34:54,040 --> 00:34:56,020
the Erie Canal was a success.

418
00:34:56,021 --> 00:35:05,160
And as traffic grew, a new vision of the canal was shaping up, twice as wide, built of stone and mortar.

419
00:35:05,880 --> 00:35:10,660
A canal that could keep up with the advances in 19th century technology.

420
00:35:14,180 --> 00:35:23,440
Mules were preferred over horses to pull canal boats because they were considered smarter, less likely to scare, and less likely to drink contaminated canal water.

421
00:35:23,441 --> 00:35:26,640
The Erie Canal will return on Modern Marvels.

422
00:35:28,840 --> 00:35:35,560
By 1836, the Erie Canal was hailed as an unqualified success for New York and for America.

423
00:35:36,600 --> 00:35:39,540
It was said that if you stood on a bridge over the canal,

424
00:35:39,860 --> 00:35:45,200
you would see a solid line of boats stretching both directions as far as the eye could see.

425
00:35:46,700 --> 00:35:49,140
Success, of course, brought its own problems.

426
00:35:49,141 --> 00:35:59,760
It didn't take very long into the history of the canal for people to start complaining about how they had to wait for a very long time when the canal broke.

427
00:36:00,080 --> 00:36:06,240
Or the canal was letting it down when it flooded, when a lock was taking too long, when a boat turned over.

428
00:36:06,720 --> 00:36:14,560
And so the canal, by being so successful, in fact, raised expectations so high that people were soon quite disappointed with it.

429
00:36:14,561 --> 00:36:21,560
In 1836, a new project began intent on rectifying the problems of the canal.

430
00:36:21,561 --> 00:36:28,560
The project would nearly double the width of the canal to 70 feet and deepen it to 7 feet.

431
00:36:28,561 --> 00:36:33,560
The new version of the canal would be lined with stone instead of dirt.

432
00:36:33,561 --> 00:36:36,560
Bridges were raised. Locks were modernized.

433
00:36:36,561 --> 00:36:42,560
But by 1862, when the enlargement project was complete,

434
00:36:42,561 --> 00:36:45,560
the Erie Canal was once again too crowded.

435
00:36:46,560 --> 00:36:50,560
Each succession of improvements only brought more business along the canal,

436
00:36:50,561 --> 00:36:54,560
which would call for another round of widening and improvements.

437
00:36:55,560 --> 00:36:58,560
When talking about the Erie Canal, it's sort of like a seamless continuum

438
00:36:58,561 --> 00:37:01,560
of constantly tweaking it and improving it.

439
00:37:01,561 --> 00:37:03,560
Well, this is too small, so we'll make it larger.

440
00:37:03,561 --> 00:37:05,560
And they call that the enlarged Erie.

441
00:37:05,561 --> 00:37:07,560
That's what you see today virtually everywhere.

442
00:37:07,561 --> 00:37:11,560
An old lock alongside a road probably is enlarged Erie Vintage.

443
00:37:11,561 --> 00:37:13,380
large Erie vintage, not Clinton's Ditch.

444
00:37:15,340 --> 00:37:19,660
In 1895, yet another improvement in widening project began.

445
00:37:20,580 --> 00:37:25,320
Under-budgeted and under-planned, it was halted when only 40% complete.

446
00:37:25,960 --> 00:37:28,620
Some said the canal had outlived its usefulness.

447
00:37:31,500 --> 00:37:37,120
For despite its success, the Erie Canal had a new rival, the railroads.

448
00:37:37,121 --> 00:37:45,000
When the canal began in 1817, the very idea of a self-propelled land vehicle on tracks was inconceivable.

449
00:37:46,120 --> 00:37:51,620
Now a railroad could carry more freight and passengers for longer distance than any canal.

450
00:37:53,680 --> 00:37:59,540
But the Erie Canal was saved by the energies of one man, Theodore Roosevelt.

451
00:38:00,420 --> 00:38:04,020
In 1899, Roosevelt, as newly elected governor of New York,

452
00:38:04,021 --> 00:38:06,400
formed a policy committee on canals,

453
00:38:06,680 --> 00:38:09,060
and announced a new approach to the old Erie.

454
00:38:09,840 --> 00:38:13,460
He even gave it a new name, the Barge Canal.

455
00:38:15,100 --> 00:38:21,300
This was going to be a canal totally different in how it operated, where it went.

456
00:38:21,600 --> 00:38:22,900
It was a totally different species.

457
00:38:23,800 --> 00:38:27,640
These were going to be barges pushed or pulled by tugboats.

458
00:38:28,160 --> 00:38:29,320
Animal power was gone.

459
00:38:30,080 --> 00:38:32,360
All the locks would generate their own electricity.

460
00:38:32,361 --> 00:38:34,840
We no longer would we use cut stone.

461
00:38:35,520 --> 00:38:37,160
It's going to be poured concrete.

462
00:38:38,220 --> 00:38:39,780
So it was an entirely different species.

463
00:38:41,120 --> 00:38:48,780
So successful was this new canal that it became the prime freight carrier for the state of New York up until the 1950s.

464
00:38:50,360 --> 00:38:57,180
In fact, Roosevelt's barge canal was almost a practice session for his better-known feet, the Panama Canal.

465
00:38:57,181 --> 00:39:05,660
The similarities between the Panama Canal and the Erie Canal don't end there.

466
00:39:06,820 --> 00:39:11,960
They poured more concrete. They blasted more rock. They excavated more soil.

467
00:39:13,160 --> 00:39:16,640
The engineering was perhaps as complex, if not more, than the Panama Canal.

468
00:39:16,641 --> 00:39:28,520
We have the flight which is locks 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6, which bring the canal from up an elevation of 168 feet.

469
00:39:29,340 --> 00:39:34,660
Now the Panama Canal, which is considered to be a wonder of the world, only raises 85 feet.

470
00:39:35,460 --> 00:39:38,280
And that whole canal is 37 miles long.

471
00:39:38,281 --> 00:39:48,160
So our system, I think, has a lot more to offer from an engineering standpoint, from a historical standpoint, than even the Panama Canal.

472
00:39:50,320 --> 00:39:59,160
Even so, by the middle of the 20th century, the combination of railroads, highways, and seaway had brought an end to the old canal.

473
00:40:00,220 --> 00:40:03,500
The Erie, first highway to the west, was dead.

474
00:40:03,501 --> 00:40:10,500
But in the years after its demise, the canal's ghost still haunted the highways of transportation.

475
00:40:10,501 --> 00:40:16,500
If you look at the Erie Canal, the track of the Erie Canal, and you look at early railroads,

476
00:40:16,501 --> 00:40:20,500
the early railroads followed the Erie Canal.

477
00:40:20,501 --> 00:40:25,500
And if you look at the New York State Thruway, that's basically running parallel to the canal as well.

478
00:40:25,501 --> 00:40:31,500
So you have three main transportation systems of America, the canals, the railroads, and the Thruway,

479
00:40:31,501 --> 00:40:34,500
all in this thin corridor going from Albany to Buffalo.

480
00:40:37,500 --> 00:40:40,500
Today, the Erie Canal has experienced a resurrection.

481
00:40:41,500 --> 00:40:43,500
In towns across New York State,

482
00:40:43,501 --> 00:40:47,500
there has been a revival of interest in America's first superhighway.

483
00:40:49,500 --> 00:40:53,500
Cities like Lockport and Rome have refurbished parts of the old canal,

484
00:40:53,501 --> 00:40:55,500
and sites like the Erie Canal Village

485
00:40:55,501 --> 00:41:00,500
recreate the scenes and feel of life in early 19th century America.

486
00:41:01,500 --> 00:41:06,240
Replicas of old canal boats will take passengers on tours from Albany to Buffalo.

487
00:41:06,980 --> 00:41:11,240
I think most people probably would be shocked to know we still have a navigable waterway.

488
00:41:11,400 --> 00:41:14,800
We have 57 locks. We have 18 lift bridges.

489
00:41:15,120 --> 00:41:18,080
There's 524 miles of navigable waterway.

490
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:23,860
I spend every weekend during the summer on the canal in my own boat,

491
00:41:24,500 --> 00:41:29,620
seeing the recreational boaters on the system, how much they enjoy it.

492
00:41:29,621 --> 00:41:36,840
And these towns now are starting to turn around and look at the canal as a source of revenue.

493
00:41:37,740 --> 00:41:41,860
You see them sprucing the town up towards the water.

494
00:41:44,160 --> 00:41:49,460
Part of this turnabout has been due to the canal being viewed as a recreational and tourist attraction,

495
00:41:49,900 --> 00:41:52,220
rather than an outmoded transportation method.

496
00:41:52,221 --> 00:42:03,552
The New York State Canal Corporation has cleaned up the water, refashioned the old mule's towpath for bikers and runners, and revamped some of the crumbling remains of

497
00:42:03,553 --> 00:42:05,180
the 19th century marvel.

498
00:42:05,181 --> 00:42:13,040
You know, after cruising on so much of the system, no pun intended, it's kind of eerie sometimes.

499
00:42:13,140 --> 00:42:20,020
You'll be going along the waterway and you'll swear around the next corner you'll hear hoofs

500
00:42:20,021 --> 00:42:23,920
or you'll see a mule-drawn barge.

501
00:42:25,200 --> 00:42:28,940
But the simplicity and calm we might feel along the canal today

502
00:42:28,941 --> 00:42:33,020
was certainly not the reaction of the actual people who built and used the canal.

503
00:42:33,960 --> 00:42:36,680
It's ironic that now this canal that we celebrate,

504
00:42:36,920 --> 00:42:39,860
because it seems like it represents a slower pace of life,

505
00:42:39,940 --> 00:42:44,200
it seems as though it was a time when life was a lot less complicated.

506
00:42:44,660 --> 00:42:48,780
In fact, at the time, it was seen as being something that was complicating people's lives,

507
00:42:48,820 --> 00:42:51,960
and that was a sign of progress and in no way a sign of the past.

508
00:42:53,420 --> 00:42:57,700
There is perhaps a surprising lesson in the Erie Canal for our world today,

509
00:42:57,701 --> 00:43:02,700
when technological and social change seems to make us dizzy with its speed.

510
00:43:03,020 --> 00:43:07,920
Well, I think that the Erie Canal, very much like any technological advances that we see

511
00:43:07,921 --> 00:43:11,627
today, did have the effect of raising people's expectations.

512
00:43:11,628 --> 00:43:13,480
And so that I think we can see

513
00:43:13,481 --> 00:43:15,747
that with anything in our own lifetime.

514
00:43:15,748 --> 00:43:18,420
That progress by its very essence does seem to

515
00:43:18,421 --> 00:43:23,240
raise people's expectations. And it often comes with consequences that people don't

516
00:43:23,241 --> 00:43:29,120
like and that people need to be aware potentially that this is not a new story that we're living

517
00:43:29,121 --> 00:43:33,420
today with the Internet, that it's something that's a very old story, in fact.

518
00:43:35,280 --> 00:43:41,820
The Erie Canal, founded at nearly the beginning of the American Republic, still going strong

519
00:43:41,821 --> 00:43:47,880
today, is a symbol of the enduring yet adaptable quality of human technology and ingenuity.

520
00:43:49,020 --> 00:43:54,520
Despite the changes of nearly 200 years, it may still be true that you'll always know

521
00:43:54,521 --> 00:43:56,700
your neighbor, you'll always know your pal

522
00:43:56,701 --> 00:43:58,700
if you've ever navigated

523
00:43:58,701 --> 00:44:00,220
on the Erie Canal.

